 ],
 "notes": "The list has 36 stations on lines 736–773. 25 are starred, which matches the other reader's count. Longstone is the only one with †, and it has no star. The list has no map numbers, light characters or sub-headings, so every row's coast is the section heading 'EAST COAST OF ENGLAND' with its final full stop left off.\n\nHow I recorded things:\n- Fragments: I removed each line's leading indentation but kept the spacing inside the line exactly as printed. The Corton star (line 757) is indented one space further than the other stars, so it lines up with the unstarred names. This looks like a typesetting slip; I still counted it as a star.\n- Keepers: I left off the full stop at the end of each name and the joining 'and' in the two-keeper rows. The fragments keep them as printed.\n- Braces: Gull (lines 766–767) and Southsand Head (lines 768–769) each have a '{' on both keeper lines. It groups the two keepers of one station rather than grouping stations, so I didn't put it in marks; it only appears in the fragments.\n- Places: For 'Casquets (Alderney)' and 'Hanois (Guernsey)' I put the bracketed island in place. I kept 'No. 5 Buoy, Teesmouth' and 'Spurn (Newsand)' whole as names, because the locality and the light-vessel name can't be cleanly separated. 'No. 5' is part of the name, not a map number. 'Whitby, High,' keeps its commas.\n- Inner Farne: here it is printed 'Inner Farne', not 'Farn, Inner'.\n\nSpellings copied as printed: 'Hasbrough' (the usual spelling is Haisborough/Happisburgh; it appears as both an L.V. and an L.H.), 'Newarp', 'Orfordness', 'Southsand Head', 'Eastside' and 'Leman and Ower'. Some keeper names may be misprints worth checking against other years' lists: 'Thomas Randule' (Randall?), 'J. G. Fornman' (Fordman/Forman?), 'Samuel Sheet' and 'Charles Perfrement'.\n\nCasquets and Hanois are Channel Islands lights, but they are printed under this east coast list.",
